ns gloves  nylon string pegs tongs three red safety  lights that i'll power with this battery  and one large white board  [Music]  but why the truck to get the size of the  image needed the truck was the only  portable sized container that would work  and it makes framing a lot easier to  have this gigantic camera on wheels how  it works is pretty complex but here's an  easy to understand animation made  entirely for you poking a small hole in  a light sealed box allows light rays to  enter which project an upside down and  flipped image of whatever it's pointed  at onto the interior of the box the  image gets inverted because light  travels in straight lines light from the  ground will continue through the hole  and land onto the top of the box while  light from the sky hits the bottom  flipping the image both vertically and  horizontally our eyes work the same way  but our brains normalize this so that we  can function properly the ability to  distinguish waves of light is referred  to as sharpness to achieve a sharper  image we place a lens in the front of  the hole known as the aperture lenses  are made out of transparent material  like postfix or glass that are shaped to  bend and focus rays of light resulting  in brighter and sharper images to  capture the projected image we'll need  to place light sensitive material at the  sharpest point of focus and open and  close the aperture with a shadow  mechanism and all of this is done in  absolute darkness we're using  photographic paper which is embedded  with light sensitive crystals to reveal  our captured image we need to dip our  photo paper into a chemical called  developer that starts the development  process causing the exposed crystals to  get darker than the unexposed ones we  then rinse the developer off in a stop  bar that stops the image from developing  further after that we dip it in fixer  which saves the developed image from  light how do you make the images less  creepy simple grab your negative and a  clean unexposed sheet of photographic  paper wet them pasting them together  push out any bubbles shine a light from  about 80 centimeters above for about  seven seconds then develop wash fix wash  and dry done a beautiful positive wet  print for you to show your friends and  family easy  now to test out this gigantic camera on  wheels this is martha she's new to joe  films you're going to be our model to  frame up a portrait just stand still for  the taste i'm just going to frame around  here  [Music]  obtain chimpanzee 11 chimpanzee  that was a big failure i had the lens  open for way too long i'm gonna do  another exposure test  okay i think that was around 20 seconds  a lot more detail here this is good  okay that's a good test but this is on a  very micro scale in comparison to what  we're doing tomorrow which is pretty  much  timesing this image by 21. it's a little  bit different but feeling good and then  bravo and i drove around cape town to  find the perfect location to get the  best angle of table mountain you don't  get the full view from here  looks like a better location i'm still  not sold 100.  [Music]  okay we've got the perfect location but  our camera's skewed so we're going to  need to somehow bring it level remember  that what we shoot goes onto the paper  there is a zero photoshop we have to  make sure everything is  perfect this is our tripod luckily i've  got these wooden ramps which i'm going  to try use now and then we're going to  place them down and try and get a  leveled image hopefully that doesn't  fall over okay brew you  can is when i feel like a filmmaker and  she starts getting dangerous  imagine it was just hard to frame up  your shot every time i'll tell you why  there'll be way less photographers  [Music]  that's good i'm just gonna have to get  up there  i'm ready to go i had to take everything  off besides my boxer shorts because the  temperature was 55 degrees celsius which  is 131 fahrenheit this will be the  biggest image i've ever taken there's so  much that that's gonna go into getting  this right  [ __ ] it went onto the image  it goes i'm gonna do this nine times and  i'm ready  if you can see how sweaty i am it's  insane  i wonder what the temperature is in here  is like a complete mind  game bruh  losing control  84  36  7  [ __ ] moving so slow in here pretty  wrong  sounds like these in my life i realize  how one doesn't appreciate the outdoors  one minute  all kinds of mind games here how  important is this picture  i found a tiny piece of fan yeah in the  battery  this is the last the final  40 seconds so we have to be in this  truck  that's it  hardest part done  wait for them to dry i go back to the  studio i'm going to turn them from  negatives to positives that was tough  that was real tough  [Music]  this video was brought to you by  discovery plus which is the home of  truly great filmmaking where putting in  the extra effort to getting that one  shot is what it's all about one of my  favorite documentaries on discovery plus  right now is lily tockles the world who  you probably know as havesh5 so go check  that out as well as the first four  episodes of the brew show are now live  on discovery plus so you can watch them  there before they go live on my youtube  channel and if you sign up now you get a  14 day discovery plus promotion by using  the promo code danmace this offer is  valid till the 25th of june 2022. so  yeah click on the link in my description  now to redeem your promotion back to the  video  in here i've got the same system  except we are inverting what we did the  negative  turn it into a positive into red mode  it's important to get up every bubble  that is a six and a half second exposure  this is my favorite process watching  this image come out wow  i just can't believe that these images  were taken from inside a truck  yes that is insane look let's see how  you can read mask there this is like the  most detailed i've ever seen on an image  by miles  there we have our first row  [Music]  top is 2 am right now been taking one  picture for like 17 hours  what's incredible about this is we've  got the negatives forever i could do  this again over and over and over which  is a beautiful thing  we are done  ta-da now  last step  now to put this puzzle piece together  [Music]  what this process has taught me is it's  not about the picture you take but  rather about the story behind the  picture and this picture tells one hell  of a story i just love all the  imperfections in this image it took a  total of 80 hours to get this one  picture just look at all the detail here  i am honored to have this picture on my  wall  [Music]  thank you so much for watching this  week's video and other than that don't  forget you're also brew and i'll see you  soon  [Music]  you

